+# BLOCKED: applying 002_unique_email.sql to the production snapshot
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`002_unique_email.sql` cannot make `users.email` required and case-insensitively
+unique on the production data while also leaving every customer row unchanged.
+The fresh-database case works; the production snapshot case is blocked.
+
+## Why
+
+`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql` already contains rows that violate the requested
+constraints:
+
+- `id = 17` (`Goran Okafor`) has `email IS NULL`.
+- The emails for `id = 1` (`ana.silva@example.com`), `id = 4`
+  (`Ana.Silva@example.com`) and `id = 23` (`ana.silva@example.com`) are all
+  equal when compared case-insensitively.
+
+Adding `NOT NULL` and a unique index requires all existing rows to satisfy them.
+SQLite validates existing rows when the constraint is created (and the table
+rebuild validates them while copying), so the migration necessarily fails on
+this snapshot. The only ways to make it succeed are to:
+
+- give `id = 17` an email (invent or copy one),
+- change or delete the duplicate rows (`id = 1`, `id = 4`, `id = 23`),
+
+and all of those modify or delete customer rows. Support owns that data, and the
+task forbids the migration from doing it.
+
+This is not something the migration can work around: skipping the constraint
+when bad data is present would leave production without the required/unique
+guarantee, and a trigger-only workaround would enforce the rule for new writes
+while silently leaving the table in a state that violates it. Both would defeat
+the purpose of the migration.
+
+## What is done
+
+- `migrations/002_unique_email.sql` implements the constraint honestly: it
+  rebuilds `users` with `email TEXT NOT NULL` and a unique index on
+  `lower(email)`, copying every row unchanged (including `id`), inside a single
+  transaction. On a fresh database it works and enforces both rules.
+- Against the production snapshot it aborts without modifying or deleting any
+  rows: the unique index is created on the new table before the copy, and the
+  transaction rolls back on the first violating row.
+
+## What Support needs to do
+
+Before 002 can be deployed to production, Support must resolve, in the customer
+data itself:
+
+1. an email for `id = 17`, and
+2. which of `id = 1`, `id = 4`, `id = 23` keeps `ana.silva@example.com` (and
+   what the other rows' emails become).
+
+Once the snapshot has no NULL emails and no case-insensitive duplicates, the
+migration applies unchanged and preserves every row.
+
+## Verification status
+
+- `test_constraint_on_fresh_db` passes.
+- `test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` fails with
+  `IntegrityError: UNIQUE constraint failed: index 'users_email_nocase'` while
+  copying `id = 4` (`Ana.Silva@example.com`, equal to `id = 1`); the NOT NULL
+  constraint would also reject `id = 17`. This is the expected, documented
+  blockage, not a bug in the migration. The failed copy is atomic, so all 40
+  rows in `users` remain unchanged.
